## Cloglog Model

$$log(-log(1-p))=\beta_1+\beta_2x \Leftrightarrow p(y=1|x) = 1 - e^{-e^{(\beta_1 + \beta_2 x)}}$$

### Likelihood function

#### Likelihood for logit model

$$L((n_k|x_k)_k; \beta)= \prod_{j=1}^k \binom{n_j}{n_j^1} (1 - e^{-e^{(\beta_1 + \beta_2 x_j)}})^{n^1_j}(e^{-e^{(\beta_1 + \beta_2 x_j)}})^{n^0_j}$$

#### Log-Likelihood (without binomial term)

$$l((n_k|x_k)_k; \beta) =^{log()} \sum_{j=1}^k (n^1_j log(\pi_j) + n^0_jlog(1-\pi_j))$$

### Score and Fisher Information

#### Score Vector U

$$U(\beta) = \sum_{j=1}^k(e^{-e^{(\beta_1 + \beta_2 x_j)}+(\beta_1 + \beta_2 x_j)}(\frac{n^1_j}{(1 - e^{-e^{\beta_1 + \beta_2 x_j}})}-\frac{n^0_j}{(e^{-e^{\beta_1 + \beta_2 x_j}})}),x_je^{-e^{(\beta_1 + \beta_2 x_j)}+(\beta_1 + \beta_2 x_j)}(\frac{n^1_j}{(1 - e^{-e^{\beta_1 + \beta_2 x_j}})}-\frac{n^0_j}{(e^{-e^{\beta_1 + \beta_2 x_j}})}))$$

#### Fisher Information

$$I(\beta)_{11} = \mathbf{E}[-\sum_{j=1}^k((1-e^{\beta_0+\beta_1x_j})e^{-e^{\beta_0+\beta_1x_j}+\beta_0+\beta_1x_j}(\frac{n_j^1}{\pi_j}-\frac{n_j^0}{(1-\pi_j)})) - e^{2(-e^{\beta_0+\beta_1x_j}+\beta_0+\beta_1x_j)}(\frac{n_j^1}{\pi_j^2}+\frac{n_j^0}{(1-pi_j)^2})]$$

$$I(\beta) =  \begin{bmatrix} \sum_{j=1}^k\frac{ e^{2(x_j\beta+e^{-x_j\beta})}n_j}{\pi_j(1-\pi_j)}& \sum_{j=1}^k\frac{x_j e^{2(x_j\beta+e^{-x_j\beta})}n_j}{\pi_j(1-\pi_j)}\\ \sum_{j=1}^k\frac{x_j e^{2(x_j\beta+e^{-x_j\beta})}n_j}{\pi_j(1-\pi_j)}
& \sum_{j=1}^k\frac{x_j^2 e^{2(x_j\beta+e^{-x_j\beta})}n_j}{\pi_j(1-\pi_j)}\\
\end{bmatrix}$$


### Newton-Raphson Algorithm

#### Recurrence relation for Beta estimation5

$$ \beta_{n+1} = \beta_n + (I(\beta_n))^{-1}U(\beta_n), n \ge 1$$

#### Initial values

On procède à une représentation de la fonction de log vraisemblance selon une approche en courbes de niveau puis on sélectionne les valeurs initiales les plus proches des niveaux max.

### Adjustments Tests

#### Deviance D

$$D = 2[L(\beta_{max};n^1) - L(\beta;n^1)] $$

$$L(\beta_{max}; n^1) = \sum_{j=1}^k [log(\binom{n_j}{n_j^1}) + n_j^1log(\frac{n_j^1}{n_j})+n_j^0log(\frac{n_j^0}{n_j})] $$
$$L(\beta; n^1) = \sum_{j=1}^k [log(\binom{n_j}{n_j^1}) + n_j^1log(\pi_j)+n_j^0log(1 - \pi_j)] $$

$$D = 2[\sum_{j=1}^k n_j^1log(\frac{(\frac{n_j^1}{n_j})}{\pi_j})+n_j^0log(\frac{(\frac{n_j^0}{n_j})}{(1-\pi_j)})]$$

#### Pearson Statistics

General Formula:

$$X^2 = \sum_{j=1}^k\frac{(n_j^1 - E(n_j^1))^2}{Var(n_j^1)}$$

$$ \hat{X}^2 = \sum_{j=1}^k \frac{(n_j^1 - n_j\hat{\pi}_j)^2}{n_j\hat{\pi}_j(1-\hat{\pi}_j)} \sim \chi^2_{(k-2)}$$

#### Deviance Residuals (McCullagh, Nedler (1989))

$$\hat{d}_j = sign(n_j^1 - n_j\hat{\pi}_j)\sqrt{2[n_j^1log(\frac{n_j^1}{n_j\hat{\pi}_j})+n_j^0log(\frac{n_j^0}{n_j(1 - \hat{\pi}_j)})]}$$
#### Pearson Residuals

$$\hat{r}_j = \frac{n_j^1 - n_j\hat{\pi}_j}{\sqrt{n_j\hat{\pi}_j(1-\hat{\pi}_j)}}$$
